<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Cleartext HTTP has to be allowed, and this is a deliberate, documented
  decision rather than an oversight.

  Since API 28 Android blocks cleartext by default. That default is right for
  most apps and wrong for this one: IPTV providers overwhelmingly serve
  playlists, the Xtream API, XMLTV feeds and the streams themselves over plain
  http, frequently on a bare IP with a non-standard port and no certificate at
  all. With the platform default in place, the very first thing a user does —
  paste the URL from their subscription email — fails with
  "CLEARTEXT communication not permitted", which is not a message they can act
  on. Every player in this category permits cleartext for the same reason.

  What the user gives up is confidentiality of their playlist URL and,
  for Xtream sources, the credentials embedded in stream paths, against an
  attacker on the same network. That is a real cost. It is mitigated where it
  can be: the app never *downgrades* an https URL, credentials are encrypted at
  rest with a Keystore key (see CredentialCrypto), and the diagnostics log
  redacts them. It cannot be avoided outright without breaking the app's
  primary function.

  If you are building a variant for a provider you control and that provider is
  https-only, delete this file and the android:networkSecurityConfig attribute
  in the manifest — nothing else depends on it.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
            <!-- User-installed CAs are trusted too: TV boxes on a home network
                 sometimes sit behind a proxy the household installed, and
                 refusing those certificates breaks playback for no security
                 benefit given cleartext is already permitted above. -->
            <certificates src="user" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
